### What is this question really testing?

The interviewer is checking logistical fit and whether you will actually show up. They want confirmation that you can realistically commit to their working model and that you are a professional who adapts to the team's needs.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class LogisticalFit {
    enum Verdict { FIT, MISMATCH }

    public static Verdict check(boolean canCommit, boolean isHonest) {
        return (canCommit && isHonest) ? Verdict.FIT : Verdict.MISMATCH;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(check(true, true));   // FIT
        System.out.println(check(false, false)); // MISMATCH
    }
}
```

### How do you understand their model before answering?

Ask or confirm: on-site means daily, hybrid means a set number of days, remote means location-independent. Match your answer to their reality.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class WorkModels {
    enum Model { ON_SITE, HYBRID, REMOTE }

    public static String adapt(Model model) {
        return switch (model) {
            case ON_SITE -> "I am comfortable being in the office daily.";
            case HYBRID  -> "Three days in office works well for me.";
            case REMOTE  -> "I am set up for remote work with a reliable home office.";
        };
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(adapt(Model.HYBRID));
    }
}
```

```text
Three days in office works well for me.
```

### How do you frame in-office time positively?

Present the in-office days as a benefit for collaboration, not a cost. Design discussions, incident coordination, and team rapport are genuinely better in person.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.List;

public class InOfficeValue {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> benefits = List.of(
            "Design discussions: whiteboard + real-time feedback",
            "Incident coordination: faster communication during crises",
            "Team rapport: relationships built over shared meals",
            "Mentoring: pair programming is better in person"
        );
        System.out.println("In-office benefits:");
        benefits.forEach(b -> System.out.println("  - " + b));
    }
}
```

### How do you surface a constraint constructively?

If you have a relocation timeline or a caregiving responsibility, state it clearly and early with a plan. Hiding a constraint is like suppressing an exception : the problem surfaces later at a worse time.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class ConstraintHandling {
    public static String surfaceConstraint(String constraint, String plan) {
        return "Constraint: " + constraint + ". Plan: " + plan + ".";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(surfaceConstraint(
            "I need 4 weeks to relocate",
            "I can start remote immediately and be on-site by week 5"
        ));
    }
}
```

```text
Constraint: I need 4 weeks to relocate. Plan: I can start remote immediately and be on-site by week 5.
```

### Why should you never over-promise?

Saying yes to a model you cannot sustain only creates a problem in three months. Honest flexibility builds trust; over-promising erodes it.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class OverPromiseRisk {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Over-promise: 'I can be on-site 5 days' (but cannot sustain it)");
        System.out.println("Result: breaks in 3 months, damages trust");
        System.out.println("Honest: 'I can do 3 days; on-site 5 is not sustainable for me'");
        System.out.println("Result: clear expectations, no surprises");
    }
}
```

### How do you show you understand why the model matters for a payments team?

A payments team at National Bank of India may need in-person coverage during incident-heavy periods. Acknowledging that signals maturity.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class TeamContext {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("I understand a payments team needs in-person coverage during incidents.");
        System.out.println("I am happy to be present when the team needs it, "
            + "not just when the policy requires it.");
    }
}
```

### What practical details should you confirm?

Ask about core hours, time-zone overlap for remote work, and office location. Both sides need a complete picture to avoid surprises.

```java
package com.bank.interview;

import java.util.List;

public class PracticalDetails {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<String> toConfirm = List.of(
            "Core hours: what time must I be available?",
            "Time-zone: any overlap requirement for remote days?",
            "Office location: which building or campus?",
            "Flexibility: are the in-office days fixed or flexible?"
        );
        toConfirm.forEach(System.out::println);
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Expect: "Can you start immediately?" (give an honest notice period), "Are you open to travel?" (be honest about frequency), and "What is your ideal work setup?" (describe productivity preferences without making demands).

```java
package com.bank.interview;

public class LocationFollowUps {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Start immediately? -> I have a 30-day notice period");
        System.out.println("Travel? -> Open to occasional travel for team events");
        System.out.println("Ideal setup? -> Hybrid: in-office for collaboration, remote for deep work");
    }
}
```
